    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Layered deepwater carbonates of the Ionian basin, Albania, show fracture and faulting patterns documenting the evolution of hierarchical structural elements that change with structural position in the Albanides fold and thrust belt. Study locations captured different structural styles ranging from low, moderate to high strain, including backlimb layered carbonates, folded beds at an anticlinal hinge and thrust and lateral tear faults respectively. Deep burial combined with the Africa-Eurasia collision from the Maastrichtian onwards resulted in mechanical layer-bounded structural features reactivated in Oligocene-Miocene time during folding and thrusting of the Apulian foreland. In the backlimb layered carbonates of Mali Gjëre and folded beds of Shpiragu we observed bed-parallel stylolites, the structures controlling mechanical layering. Layer-bounded structural features include a contemporaneous set of bed-perpendicular stylolites and veins formed while the beds were horizontal, followed by another stylolite and vein set formed due to slip on the layers with the onset of folding. These two locations show layer parallel shear and strike-slip faulting. In the Kremenara and Letan locations we see evidence of tear faulting, where complex high strain structures involving layer parallel brecciation, strike-slip faults and backthrusts show an increase in local deformation due to fault slip. Oil seeps at the tear fault locations indicate these faults acted as a migration pathway from Jurassic source rocks to the Upper Cretaceous - Eocene reservoir beds currently outcropping in the field. The migration pathway for fluids increase with proximity to large structures such as tear faults, thrusts or anticline hinges due to the increase in shear components. Tear faults are the main conduit for vertical migration with sheared bed-parallel stylolites, bed-perpendicular stylolites and veins, and a series of shear related components all interacting to form a connected network for hydrocarbon migration and storage.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
